Muscle Sprain (Grade 1-2)
Musculoskeletal & Pain
Acute pain on active contraction, Localized muscle tenderness, Pain on passive stretch

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: MKP-033
Reason for Visit: Sudden, sharp hamstring pain resulting in acute functional impairment and inability to ambulate normally.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage determined that the patient suffered an acute, high-velocity injury resulting in severe, localized thigh pain and sudden inability to walk without severe limping. Urgent assessment was necessary to rapidly differentiate a benign muscle strain from a complete musculotendinous rupture or avulsion fracture, and to implement immediate acute tissue management protocols to prevent further tearing prior to standard clinical hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I sprinted for the bus, felt a sharp tearing sensation in the back of my thigh, and my leg completely gave out from underneath me."
History of Present Illness: Patient presents with an acute onset of severe right posterior thigh pain following a sudden eccentric load. Timeline: Injury occurred 4 hours ago during a sudden, forceful sprint. Patient experienced an immediate, sharp, localized muscle pain in the mid-hamstring belly, forcing them to abruptly halt. Reports a mild to moderate localized swelling developing rapidly over the ensuing hours. The pain is consistently reproduced and severely exacerbated by any active contraction of the hamstring against gravity. Patient currently exhibits a severe antalgic movement pattern, keeping the knee bent to avoid engaging or stretching the injured muscle during functional tasks.
Functional Status: Ambulation is severely impaired; patient is hopping or dragging the affected limb to avoid hamstring activation.
Social/Safety Concerns: High fall risk due to sudden limb yielding when the muscle is inadvertently loaded.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 132/84 | HR: 90 | Temp: 36.7 | RR: 16 | SpO2: 100% | Wt: 78 Kg
General: Alert, in moderate distress when attempting to move, resting with the right knee flexed.
Physical Exam: Inspection of the right posterior thigh reveals mild localized swelling but no massive ecchymosis (yet). Palpation demonstrates exquisite tenderness explicitly confined to the mid-substance of the biceps femoris muscle belly. Crucially, there is an absence of a large palpable defect or complete loss of continuity within the muscle. Pain is severely provoked by passive stretching of the hamstring (straight leg raise limited by mechanical muscle pain at 40 degrees). Active resisted knee flexion reproduces sharp, intense pain. Motor strength is reduced (4/5) secondary to pain inhibition, but the limb remains fundamentally usable. Neurological examination is normal.
Environmental Observations: Patient is navigating a split-level home, increasing the difficulty of the impaired gait.
Medication Reconciliation: No medications taken. Applied heat initially (which is contraindicated in the acute phase).

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Acute Hamstring Muscle Strain (Grade 2) - Status: Acute
Differentials:

Complete Hamstring Rupture (Grade 3) / Ischial Tuberosity Avulsion - Ruled out clinically by the preservation of active knee flexion against gravity, absence of a massive palpable muscular defect, and lack of focal bony point tenderness at the ischial origin.

Sciatica (Lumbar Radiculopathy) - Ruled out by the specific mechanism of forceful muscular contraction, localized muscle belly tenderness, and absence of shooting, electric pain radiating below the knee.

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out by the acute, instantaneous traumatic onset, localization to the posterior thigh rather than the calf, and normal distal vascular exam.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient sustained a classic, acute eccentric overload injury resulting in a mid-substance hamstring strain. The sudden loss of function and severe pain upon movement rendered the patient functionally unsafe and highly distressed. Postponing evaluation would have exposed the patient to unmanaged pain, the continuation of inappropriate initial therapies (heat application), and the risk of converting a partial tear into a complete rupture through unassisted, improper mobilization. Urgent after-hours assessment safely confirmed structural continuity of the muscle, bypassing the immediate need for emergency imaging, and permitted the immediate initiation of strict rest, ice, compression, and proper mobility aids.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a 5-day course of Paracetamol 1g QID and Ibuprofen 400mg TDS with food. Advised immediate cessation of all heat application.
Positive Behavioral Change:
Action: Apply a firm, wide compression bandage completely encompassing the thigh and utilize an ice pack for 20 minutes every 2 hours. Consequence: Immediate constriction of bleeding micro-vessels in the muscle, preventing the buildup of a massive, painful hematoma and reducing the throbbing ache (negative reinforcement).
Action: Use the provided crutches for all ambulation for the next 48 hours, maintaining partial weight-bearing. Consequence: Mechanical offloading allows the torn muscle fibers to physically approximate and begin scarring together without being repeatedly ripped apart by walking (positive reinforcement).
Home-Based Services (Optional): Physiotherapy referral mandated for progressive loading and rehabilitation to prevent chronic re-injury.
DME/Safety (Optional): Supplied standard axillary crutches and compression bandage.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 5-7 days to assess functional recovery and guide transition to physical therapy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate ED presentation if the entire leg becomes massively swollen, tight, and numb, or if the foot turns cold and pale.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis is clinical, representing a partial muscle tear. Ultrasound imaging is only required if recovery fails to progress. Conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The acute pain will settle over 3-5 days. A large, dark bruise will likely appear and track down toward the knee over the next 48 hours. Healing requires weeks of guided strength rehabilitation to prevent a weak scar.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Seek emergency medical care if your leg swells up massively, feels incredibly tight and agonizing, or if you completely lose feeling in your foot.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: You have physically torn thousands of microscopic muscle fibers in your hamstring. Using heat increases the bleeding inside the muscle. The compression bandage and ice act as a physical tourniquet to stop the internal bleeding and limit the painful swelling. The crutches prevent you from tearing the delicate new scar tissue.
Actionable Steps: Stop using heat immediately. Switch to strict ice and compression. Use the crutches for all movement; do not try to test the muscle by walking normally until the sharp pain is entirely gone.

